In my case, the crissury motion criticised me for disagreeing with Jeremy Corbyn.
Concerning the importance of a responsible economic policy on national security on Europe, ironically similar issues on which Jeremy disagreed with previous leaders.
Notification to the Nottingham East Labour meeting on Friday mentioned that "we want the meetings to be inclusive and productive".
For the majority of my eight years as local labor representative in parliament, the meetings of the General Committee have just been just that.
“Unfortunately, it is no longer the tone of many of the gatherings and the promise of “more kind and kinder” politics has long been forgotten, if it has ever materialized.
It has become more and more apparent that different points of view are not tolerated in the Labor Party, and that each opinion is judged on whether or not it is acceptable by party leadership.
This began a short time after Jeremy took the lead, because colleagues with whom he thought he shared a similar political perspective began to await me for a turn and who would assume positions with which he would never agree - either on national security or on the EU's single market.
Every time I speak in public (and it does not really matter what I say), there follows a torrent of insults on social networks that call for "displacement", denouncing the policies of the center, telling me that I must not belong to the Labour Party.
It’s not just me who goes through it.
In fact, I know that I am more fortunate than some of my colleagues, since the comments addressed to me tend to be of a political nature.
I’m astonished by the professionalism and determination of these colleagues who face a dam of sexist or racist slurs every day that never back down to them.
One of the aspects that disillusions me most in this political era is the way these levels of slurs have become normalised.
Jeremy Corbyn said last week that Labour should promote a culture of tolerance.
The reality is that we have ceased to be even inclusive, and with every "censoration" or modification of the rules of selection the party narrows its views.
“I’ve received innumerable advice over the last two years urging me to keep my head down, not to be so expressive, and that ‘everything would be okay’ for me.
But that's not what I've come to do politics for.
Ever since I joined the Labor Party 32 years ago as a student, impelled by the negligence of Thatcher's government that left the classroom of my comprehensive school literally in debris, I've tried to fight for better public services for those who most need from them, either as a mayor or a minister in the government.
“I’ve never concealed my policies, including in recent elections.
“None in Nottingham East could have been somewhat confused as to my political positions and areas of disagreement with the current leadership.
“To those who promoted the motion on Friday, all I have to say is that when the country is headed for a Brexit that will harm families, businesses and our public services, I don’t understand the desire to lose time and energy on the subject of my alloy to the Leader of the Labour Party.
But in fact, the message I have is not for the Nottingham Momentum; it’s for my constituents, members of the Labour Party or not: I’m proud to serve you and I promise you that no “redelicts” or political expediving threats will prevent me from acting in the sense of what I believe is the interests of all of you.
