With more potential job opportunities that require knowledge of languages, these skills are essential in a globalised world.
Whatever career they have chosen, if they have learned a second language they will have a tangible advantage in the future with an actheistary for life like this.
The ability to communicate directly with people from foreign countries will automatically place a multi-lingual person ahead of the competition.
According to a YouGov survey of more than 4,000 adults in the UK in 2013, 75 per 75 per cent did not speak a foreign language well enough to maintain a conversation, with French being the only language spoken by a two-digit ratio.
Investing now in language teaching is important for today’s children.
Multi-Language dominance, in particular those of developing countries, will give children who have a better chance of relevancy.
In Scotland every school differs in the languages it teaches.
Some of them will focus on the most classical modern languages, while others will teach languages considered to be those most important to the United Kingdom for the horizon of 2020, such as mandarin or janiculum.
Whatever your child's interest, there will always be a set of languages to be chosen in self-employed schools, supported by specialized teaching staff in the area.
Scottish self-employed are committed to providing an educational environment that prepares children and gives them the necessary aptitudes for their success, whatever the future has.
Undeniably, today, in a globalised business environment, languages continue to be of fundamental importance for the future of the country, so it needs to be reflected in education.
