Students at Harvard attend course that teaches them to rest enough
A new course at Harvard University this-year has put all of its students to sleep more, in an initiative to combat sexist culture of studying in ‘directions’ impregnated with caffeine.
One professor concluded that students of this university at the top of the world ranking often did not have the slightest idea about the most basic concepts about the care they should take with themselves.
Charles Czeisler, Professor of Sleep Medicine at Harvard Medical School and Specialist at Brigham and Women's Hospital, has authored the course, which he believes to be the first of its kind in the United States.
Inspiration for the course acquiesced after giving a talk about the impact of sleep deprivation on learning.
"At the end a girl came up with me and said, 'Why are you just saying this to me in the last year of the course?'
“The girl stated that no one had ever mentioned to her the importance of sleep, which surprised me,” she told The Telegraph.
The course, delivered for the first time this year, explains to students the essential concepts about how good sleep habits contribute positively to academic and athletic performance, as well as the improvement of general well-bea-enhancing.
Paul Barreira, professor of psychiatry at Harvard Medical School and Executive Director of Health Services at the university, stated that the institution has decided to introduce the course after concluding that some students suffered from severe sleep deprivation throughout the week.
One-Hour Course includes a number of Interactive Tasks.
In one section there's an image of a dormitory where the students click in coffee mugs, curtains, sneakers and books to be informed about the effects of caffeine and light, and on how athletic performance is influenced by sleep deficiency, and the importance of a routine to collect to bed.
In another section attendees are briefed on how long-term sleep deprivation may increase the risks of C steak, depression and cancer.
Afterwards, a campus map, with interactive icons, encourages attendees to think of their daily routines.
"We know it won't change students' behavior instantaneously.
" However, we believe they have a right to know, just like we all have a right to know the health effects of the smoking option," Czeisler added.
Pride culture in “doing directs” still exists, he said, adding that modern technology and ever-increasing pressure on students make it a growing problem.
Enasuring that we sleep well and with quality should be a “secrete weapon” of students to combat stress, depletion and anxiety, he said, and even to avoid fattening because sleep deprivation puts the brain into starvation, making them constantly starving.
Raymond So, a 19-Year-Old Physiological Student of Biology and Chemistry, helped Professor Czeisler design the course after having attended one of his classes last year at Harvard.
“The course opened his eyes and inspired him to incentivize the creation of a course for the whole campus,” he said.
“The next step, according to their expectations, is to ask all post-graduate students to attend a similar program of studies before entering the competitive institution.
Professor Czeisler has recommended students to consider the use of an alarm for the time to go to bed, such as to wake up, and to be aware of the harmful effects of "blue light" emitted by electronic screens and LED lighting, which can deregulate circa-Chlodian rhythm and lead to trouble falling asleep.
