VIH/IA-LIFAA: China Reports 14% Increase of New Cases
China reported a 14 per cent increase in the number of its citizens living with HIV and AIDS.
More than 8,20 000 people are affected in the country, health officials said.
Around 40,000 new cases were reported only in the second trimester of 2018.
The vast majority of new cases result from transmissions through sexual relations, which marks a change in relation to the past.
VIH has spread rapidly in some parts of China as a result of transfusions of infected blood.
However, the number of people who contracted VIH in this way was reduced to almost zero, the Chinese health authorities said at a conference in Yunnan province.
However, in terms of year-of-the-ordinary variation, the number of those living with HIV and AIDS in China has increased by 100 000 people.
Transmission of VIH via sexual intercourse is an acute problem in the Chinese LGBT community.
Homomosexuality was de-criminised in China in 1997, but it is said that discrimination against the LGBT community is frequent.
Studies have estimated that, due to the country's conservative values, 70 to 90% of men who have had sex with other men end up marrying with women.
Many transmissions of the diseases are derived from inadequate sexual protection in these relationships.
Since 2003, the Chinese government has pledged universal access to anti-HIV medication as part of an effort to resolve the problem.
