Inventory of the World Wide Web aims to create a new Internet to compete with Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, an inventor of the World Wide Web, created a start-verit that aims to feud with Facebook, Amazon, and Google.
The most recent project of this legendary tech character, Inrupt, is a company that takes advantage of Berners-Lee’s open-column-pronomous platform.
It allows users to decide where their data is stored and who has access to what information.
In an all-exclusive Fast Company interview, Berners-Lee judiciously stated that the underlying intention of Inrupt is to "dominate the world".
“We’ve got to do it now,” he told the start-up.
"It's a historic moment."
The application uses Solid technology to enable people to create their own "personal online data warehouse," or POD.
This may contain contacts lists, to-to-do lists, calendars, music library, and other personal and professional tools.
It's as if Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack, and Spotify were all available in one browser and at the same time.
What's unique about the online personal data warehouse is that it's only up to the user to decide on who has access to what kind of information.
The company calls it "Permanent training through data."
“Innco’s idea, according to company CEO John Bruce, is to provide adequate resources, processes and expertise to help make Solid available to everyone.
The company is currently made up of Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform purchased by IBM, a few in-house programmers hired to work on the project, and a community of programmers on a voluntary basis.
Starting this week, technology developers around the world will be able to create their own decentralised applications using the tools available on the Inrupt website.
Berner-Lee stated that he and his team aren’t speaking to “Facebook and Google about whether or not to introduce a total change where all of their business models are swirled from one day to another.
"We're not asking them for permission."
In a Medium post Saturday, Berners-Lee wrote that "the Inrupt mission is to create commercial energy and an ecosystem that will help to protect the integrity and the quality of the new Web created with Solid."
In 1994, Berners-Lee transformed the Internet when he created the Consortium of the World Wide Web at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
In recent months Berners-Lee has been an influential voice in the debate over Internet neutrality.
While releasing Inrupt, Berners-Lee will remain founder and director of the Consortium of the World Wide Web, the Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
"I'm amazingly optimistic about this early Web era," Berners-Lee added.
