New German Warship Again Delayed
German navy’s latest frigate should have entered service in 2014 to replease Cold-Finaf-War warships. However it will be necessary to await until at least the next year, occurrence due to systems prone malfunctions and an exponentially growing cost, according to local media.
Entrance to “Rheinland-Pfalz,” the commanding ship of Baden-Wuertttemberg’s novic frigates has now been postponed until the first half of 2019, according to Die Zeit that cited a military spokesman.
It was supposed to have joined the navy in 2014, but the unsettling post-delivery problems have undermined the fate of the ambitious project.
Baden-Wuertttemberg-class vessels the navy had commissioned in 2007 will serve to repleocate the aged Bremen-class frigate.
It is known that they will be equipped with a potent cannon in addition to a number of anti-aerial and anti-vessel missiles, as well as some stealthy technologies such as the reduction of radar, infrared and acoustic signatures.
Reciproximate characteristics include more dilated periods between maintenance, which would make it possible to send the latest frigates on missions with a maximum duration of two years away from ports of origin.
However, the continued delays have caused state-to-de-a-de-de-nine warships, regarded as a way for Germany to project power across cross-offers, will already be outdated when they enter into service, Die Zeit said.
The unforgivable frigate F125 made headlines last year when the German navy refused to put her into service, having returned it to Blohm & Voss shipyards in Hamburg.
It was the first time the navy had returned a ship to the yard after delivery.
Little is known about the reasons for the demise, but German media scite a number of crucial "charter and software defects" that would render the warship useless if used on a combat mission.
Deficiencies of the software were particularly important since the Baden-Wuertttemberg-class ships will be operated by a crew of about 120 seafarers, only half the contingent needed in the oldest Bremen-class frigate.
It has also become known that the vessel is excessively burdensome, which reduces its performance and limits navy's ability to install additional functionalities in the future.
Rheinland-Pfalz of 7,000 tones is believed to have twice the weight of similar-class vessels used by Germans in World World War II.
In addition to flaws of the hardware, the price of the entire project, including the training of the crew, is also becoming a problem.
It is said to have reached some staggering EUR 3.1 billion, an increase compared to 2.2 billion initially budgeted.
The problems ravaging recent frigates have become particularly important in the light of the recent warnings that German naval power is weakening.
Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Bartels, head of Germany’s parliamentary defense commission, recognized that the navy is currently running out ships capable of being used.
The politician stated that the problem has grown exponentially over time, as they were put out of service older ships without vessels to replace them.
In the end he lamented that none of the frigates of the Baden-Wuerttemberg could join the navy.
National Trust peeks into the secret life of the bats
New investigation is being carried out at a property in the Scottish Highlands to reveal how bats use the landscape to search for food.
It is expected that the results will shine some light on the behavior of these unique flying mammals and help guide conservation activities in the future.
The study, carried out by scientists at the National Trust for Scotland, will follow ordinary bats and fruit, as well as long-eared brown and Daubenton mulkeys in the Inverewe Gardens in Wester Ross.
Special engraveners will be placed in key locations scattered throughout the property to follow the activities of the bats throughout the station.
Mobile studies will also be carried out by NTS personnel and volunteers with portable detectors.
Analyzing by experts of all the recordings will assess the frequency of bat calls and what each species is doing.
A map of the habitat will then be produced to create a detailed image of its behaviors on a territorial scale.
Rob-Dewar, NTS Nature Conservation Advis, expects the results to reveal which areas of the habitat are most important for bats and how they are used by each species.
This data will help to determine the benefits of habitat management works, such as the creation of meadows, as well as the best way to maintain forests for bats and associated species.
The populations of bats in Scotland and throughout the United Kingdom decreased considerably over the last century.
These animals are endangered by construction and development work that affect the dormitories and de-escalate habitat.
Wire turbines and lightning also constitute a threat, together with the fly-killing papers and some chemical treatments of construction materials in addition to domestic cat blosprick attacks.
The bats are not blind.
However, given your night-hunting habits, your ears are more useful than the eyes when it comes to catching precautions.
They use a sophisticated echo localization technique to identify insects and hurdles in their flight path.
NTS, responsible for the care of over 2,170 historical buildings, 38 major gardens and 76,000 hectares of land throughout the region, takes the bats very seriously.
The organization counts with ten trained specialists who regularly carry out studies, dormitomber inspections and sometimes rescues.
It has also created the first reserve in Scotland dedicated exclusively to bat-tea, at the Threave estate in Dumfries and Galloway, home to eight of the ten species of Chastopian bats.
Property director David Thompson says it’s the ideal territory for them.
"Here in Threave we have an excellent area for bats," he said.
"We've got the old buildings, a lot of veteran trees and all this good habitat.
However, there’s still a great deal about the bats we don’t know, so the work we’ve been doing here and on other properties will help us to know more about what they need to flourish.”
He stressed the importance of verifying that there are bats before performing maintenance on properties, since the unintentional destruction of only one maternity dormitory can lead to the deaths of up to 400 females and young animals, which could eradicate the entire local population.
The bats are a protected species and it's illegal to kill them, harass them, disturb them or destroy their dormitories.
Elisabeth Ferrell, Scotland’s Head of the Bata Conservation Trust, encouraged the public to participate to help.
“We still have a lot to learn about our bats, and for so many of our species, we simply don’t know how their populations avenge.”
