2004: On Dec. 26, a strong quake on the west coast of the Indonesian province of Aceh in northern Sumatra originated a tsunami that hit 14 countries, killing 2,26,000 people along the Indian Ocean coast, more than half of whom were in Aceh.
2005: A series of strong earthquakes hit the western coast of Sumatra, in late March and beginning of April.
Thousands have been killed on Nias Island off Sumatra.
2006: A 6.8-magnitude quake struck south of Java, Indonesia’s most populous island, causing a tsunami that befell over the southern coast, killing almost 700 people.
2009: An earthquake of magnitude 7.6 reached the city of Padang, the capital of West Sumatra province.
Over 1,100 people have died.
2010: An earthquake of magnitude 7.5 struck one of the Mentawai islands off Sumatra, triggering a tsunami with waves of up to 10 meters that destroyed dozens of villages and killed about 300 people.
2016: Undetached quake hit the Pidie Jaya region in Aceh, spreading destruction and panic among people who remembered the devastation wrought by the deadly earthquake and tsunami of 2004.
