Up-to-date Information on Red Tide: Concentrations Decrease In Pinellas, Manatee and Sarasota
According to fresh information from the Florida Fish and Wildlife Commission, there has been a decrease in concentrations of Red Tide in some areas of Tampa Bay.
FWC reported that conditions with a lower proliferation of spots have been identified in Pinella, Manatee, Sarasota, and Collier counties, suggesting a decrease in concentrations.
The Red Tide patch extends about 201 km along the coast from Pinellas county north to Lee's county to the south.
Stains can be found about 10 miles off the Hillsborough county coast, but in fewer areas than they did last week.
Maré Vermelha was also sighted in Pasco County.
Over the past week has been recorded concentrations along or off the coast of Pinella County, low-to-tied concentrations off the coast of Hillsborough County, bottom-to-tall concentrations in Manatee County, high-bottom concentrations along or off Sarasota County’s coastline, start-to-member concentrations in Charlotte countenagement, high-rise concentrations along with or off the coast of Lee County, and low concentrations in Collier County.
Respiratory irritation continues to be reported in Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, and Collier counties.
Over the past week there have been no reports of respiratory irritations in Northwest Florida.
